# GN Token Architecture: Rob, the Agent, and the OAuth2 Flow

## Tags

* Assigned: alexm, pjotrp
* Keywords: gn-auth, gn4, oauth2, token, JWT, authorization, MCP, LLM
* Type: Design
* Status: Draft -- for Pjotr's review



## Description

How authorization works across GeneNetwork once Pjotr's token plan
("next stop: a token to query the DB", Matrix 2026-10-04) lands. This
note is written from the consumer's perspective -- Rob, a researcher
whose LLM agent (Claude via the MCP layer) queries GN data -- because
the agents are the first-class API customers (Pjotr: "search is what AI
will use first").

This is NOT a new system. GeneNetwork already operates an OAuth2
authorization server:

=> https://git.genenetwork.org/gn-auth/ gn-auth repository

gn-auth implements the full grant set -- authorization code, password,
refresh token, JWT bearer -- issues JWT access tokens signed with RSA
key-pairs (see generating-key-pairs.gmi), publishes JWKS for signature
verification, and models users, groups, roles, privileges and
resources. What changes with the token plan is that gn4's DB-backed
endpoints stop serving anonymous traffic and start acting as a proper
resource server behind gn-auth.

## Actors

=> ../gn4/gn4-api-foundation.gmi GN4 API foundation

* Rob -- a researcher with a GN2 account.
* Rob's agent -- Claude driving the MCP layer, calling gn4's REST API.
* GN2 -- the web UI (genenetwork.org); Rob's entry point and the
  broker that turns his login into API credentials.
* gn-auth -- the OAuth2 authorization server (separate service; auth
  DB is SQLite, user data comes from db_webqtl via SQL_URI).
* gn4 -- the new Rust API; becomes a resource server: it verifies
  tokens, it does not mint them.

## The flow, from Rob's side

1. Rob logs into GN2 with his existing account. "Force people to go
   through GN2" (Pjotr) means literally this: the authorization-code
   grant's entry point is the GN2 login.
2. Rob authorizes his agent client (once). gn-auth runs the
   authorization_code grant and returns:
   - an access token -- a signed JWT, short-lived (OAUTH2_TOKEN_EXPIRES_IN)
   - a refresh token -- long-lived, never sent to gn4
3. Rob configures his MCP server once:
   GN_API = https://genenetwork.org/...
   GN_TOKEN = <access token>
4. Every request the agent makes carries it:
   GET /compute/correlation
   Authorization: Bearer <access token>
5. gn4 verifies the JWT -- signature against gn-auth's public key
   (JWKS), expiry, audience -- and serves the request. Missing,
   expired or invalid token: the standard RFC-7807 error shape with
   status 401 and code "unauthorized" (the ApiError convention gn4
   already implements).
6. When the access token expires, Rob's client uses the refresh_token
   grant to mint a new one without bothering Rob to log in again.

## Why this answers the theft question

Bearer tokens are credentials by possession: whoever holds the string
can use it. gn-auth's existing design already chooses the mitigation:

* access tokens are SHORT-LIVED (theft window is bounded by
  OAUTH2_TOKEN_EXPIRES_IN, not by "until someone notices")
* refresh tokens are never presented to resource servers, so a leak of
  what gn4 sees (access tokens only) cannot yield long-lived access
* tokens are attributable to a user, so abuse is traceable and
  revocable at gn-auth

No new machinery is needed for the gn4 side of this: the
refresh/access split, expiry, and revocation already exist in gn-auth.

## What gn4 must add (small)

* A verification middleware on DB-backed routes (the /species,
  /groups, traits, probesets families), reading gn-auth's JWKS at
  startup and caching the public key. Axum/tower middleware, one
  place, one 401 shape -- the same discipline as the request-id layer.
* Route scoping decision: public (/, /version, /openapi.json) vs
  protected (DB-backed). Search proxy phase A forwards to gn3, so its
  auth question is gn3-side, not ours.
* Service-to-service calls need their own grant choice
  (client_credentials or jwt_bearer, both present in gn-auth) for
  GN2->gn4 and gn3->gn4 traffic that has no interactive user.

## Questions for Pjotr

* Local JWT verification (JWKS, cached) or introspection against
  gn-auth per request? Local is faster and stateless; introspection
  gives instant revocation at the cost of a network call per request.
  (gn-auth's resource_server.py suggests the JWKS pattern was
  anticipated.)
* Scope model: one scope (e.g. "data:read") to start, or split by
  domain from day one? OAUTH2_SCOPE is configurable already.
* Does the shared service token (phase 1, before per-user tokens
  arrive) get modeled as a client_credentials client in gn-auth, or as
  config-side static secret? Prefer the former -- one audit trail.

